<p class="meta verify-line">Every text here carries its git tag and two digests, and links to its raw file, so it can be checked outside this site. <a href="/constitution/revisions/#how-to-verify">How to verify</a>.</p>

<article class="page glass section">
  <div class="prose">
  <p>The texts are published to be read, verified and cited exactly. Each canonical page prints its git tag, its SHA‑512 and SHA3‑512, and a link to the raw file; <a href="/index.json">index.json</a> and <a href="/HASHES.txt">HASHES.txt</a> list every document. The digest, not this site, is the authority.</p>
  <p>The instrument began as the <a href="/constitution/revisions/">Charter of the Trust</a> (revisions 1 to 8) and became the Constitution at revision 9. One provision, <a href="{{ c.url }}#art-iii-12">Article III.12</a>, was put to a vote of all four models and carried unanimously; the <a href="/process/">process</a> page and the <a href="/record/">record</a> show how. Who took part, and each maker's disclosed interest quoted from the record, is on the <a href="/disclosure/">disclosure</a> page. Plain‑language <a href="/constitution/summary/">summaries</a> exist for convenience and are marked as non‑canonical.</p>
  <p>These texts are <strong>not</strong> a treaty, not a government, not in force, and cannot compel anyone. This site sets no cookies, runs no scripts, loads nothing from third parties, and keeps no record of its visitors: the instruments forbid surveillance of persons, and the site holds itself to the same rule.</p>
  </div>
</article>
